    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, what do you mean by \"feeding a hungry goblin\"? i'm just trying to figure out if i need to buy fertilizer or if the soil is just too wet. i don't really follow the gnome/goblin thing lol. can you just tell me specifically what the mushy yellow leaves mean?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies for the goblin metaphor. Let's get straight to the point. The mushy yellow leaves on your Monstera deliciosa could indicate a few things. It might be a sign of overwatering, which can lead to root rot, or it could be a nutrient deficiency. If the soil is consistently wet, the roots can become waterlogged, leading to a lack of oxygen and eventually causing the leaves to turn yellow and feel soft. On the other hand, if the soil is dry, the plant might not be getting enough nutrients, which can also cause yellowing leaves.\n\nTo address this, first, check the moisture level of the soil. If it's consistently wet, reduce your watering frequency and ensure the pot has proper drainage. If it's dry, you might need to water more regularly, but be careful not to overdo it. As for fertilizer, a balanced, slow-release fertilizer can help provide the necessary nutrients without overwhelming your Monstera. Just follow the instructions on the fertilizer package for the best results. And always remember, a little bit of care goes a long way in keeping your Monstera happy and healthy.",
